 A software-rendered 3D scene running in the desktop's content pane, on
 whatever framebuffer the firmware handed us. No host rasterizer and no
 GPU: every triangle is projected, shaded and filled by Renderer3D, and
 every pixel is a poke-32 into the panel.

 It draws into the content rectangle rather than the whole panel, so the
 sidebar, the top bar, the taskbar and the running clock stay on the glass
 around a moving view.

 The chrome sizes here are GopDesk's, and the two files have to agree by
 hand because GopDesk cites this one and the dependency cannot run the
 other way. `dk-bar-h` and `dk-task-h` are scaled by `ui-scale`; the
 sidebar's 160 is a widget minimum and is not.

 The scene is rendered into a back buffer and blitted to the pane in one
 pass rather than rasterized straight at the framebuffer. Painting in
 place is visible while it happens: the clear lands first, so a viewer
 sees the pane flash to sky and refill on every frame, and a capture taken
 mid-frame shows only the nodes drawn by then. The blit costs one read and
 one write per pixel of the pane against the clear's one write, and that
 is what the steady picture is bought with.

 The target is built once. Rebuilding it would allocate a fresh colour and
 depth buffer every frame, and at this size that is megabytes a frame that
 never come back.

 The camera orbits on the PIT tick, so the rotation runs at wall-clock
 speed whatever the frame rate. The angle goes through Cordic rather
 than Matrix4's mat4-sin-approx: that one is a two-term Taylor valid
 only near zero, and at half a turn it answers -2.03 where the sine is
 0. Cordic quadrant-reduces and is honest all the way round.

 We say:

Section: Geometry

  gsc-sidebar-w : Integer = 160
  gsc-topbar-h : Integer = 22
  gsc-taskbar-h : Integer = 28
  gsc-tick-cell : Integer = 28672

 Cordic answers on a scale of 1000 and takes milliradians, 6283 to the
 turn. The orbit radius is 5200 world units, so the scaled cosine is
 multiplied by 5.2 to land there.

  gsc-radius : Real = 5.2
  gsc-eye-height : Real = 2200.0
  gsc-turn : Integer = 6283

Section: Scene

  gsc-camera : Integer -> Camera3D
  gsc-camera (angle) =
    let ca = real-from-int (cordic-cos angle)
    in let sa = real-from-int (cordic-sin angle)
    in camera3d-new (vec3-new (ca * gsc-radius) gsc-eye-height (sa * gsc-radius)) (vec3-new 0.0 300.0 0.0) 0.785

 Built once. Only the camera changes per frame, through a record copy
 that leaves the meshes alone -- rebuilding the scene would rebuild
 every vertex and index list in it.

  gsc-scene : Integer -> Scene3DState
  gsc-scene (dummy) =
    let scene = scene3d-new (gsc-camera 0)
    in let ground = sn3-with-mesh "ground" (mesh-plane 3000 3000) (emat-flat (rgb 90 80 62))
    in let cube = sn3-set-pos (sn3-with-mesh "cube" (mesh-cube 900) (emat-shiny (rgb 90 130 210) 32)) (-900.0) 450.0 0.0
    in let pyr = sn3-set-pos (sn3-with-mesh "pyr" (mesh-pyramid 600 1100) emat-red) 1100.0 0.0 (-300.0)
    in let s2 = scene3d-add (scene3d-add (scene3d-add scene ground) cube) pyr
    in let key = dir-light (vec3-new (-400.0) 800.0 400.0) (rgb 255 250 235) 850
    in scene3d-set-ambient (scene3d-add-light s2 key) 260

Section: Loop

 The blit is the one step that has to know both pitches: the pane's rows
 sit stride apart on the panel and w apart in the back buffer.

  gsc-blit-span : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  gsc-blit-span (dst) (doff) (src) (soff) (w) (x) =
    if x >= w then 0
    else let d = poke-32 dst ((doff + x) * 4) (peek-32 src ((soff + x) * 4))
    in gsc-blit-span dst doff src soff w (x + 1)

  gsc-blit-rows : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  gsc-blit-rows (dst) (stride) (src) (w) (h) (y) =
    if y >= h then 0
    else let d = gsc-blit-span dst (y * stride) src (y * w) w 0
    in gsc-blit-rows dst stride src w h (y + 1)

 One frame: clear the back buffer, point the camera, render, blit to the
 pane, then the hint line. The hint goes onto the panel after the blit
 because the blit covers it.

  gsc-frame : R3dTriState, Scene3DState, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, Integer -> Integer
  gsc-frame (tgt) (scene) (angle) (sky) (base) (stride) (font) (ch) =
    let c = r3d-target-clear tgt sky
    in let posed = scene3d-set-camera scene (gsc-camera angle)
    in let d = r3d-render-into tgt posed
    in let b = gsc-blit-rows (base + gsc-sidebar-w * 4) stride (tgt.r3t-base) (tgt.r3t-w) (tgt.r3t-h) 0
    in gop-draw-text base stride font (gsc-sidebar-w + 8) (ch - 16) "3D: software pipeline, no GPU. Esc returns." 65280 1 0

 Esc leaves. The self tail call keeps the loop constant-stack, and the
 angle is derived from the tick counter rather than accumulated, so a
 slow frame loses no rotation and a fast one gains none.

 The frame is bracketed because there is no collector and this loop has
 no bound: a frame makes about 20 KB permanent (geometry, not pixels),
 which at the measured 36 fps is roughly 730 KB a second for as long as
 anyone leaves the view open. Everything the bracket drops is built
 inside the frame -- the posed camera, the Cordic reals, the render's
 temporaries. The target and the scene are built once in gsc-run, below
 the mark, so the restore cannot reach them, and the angle comes from
 the tick cell rather than an accumulator, so no iteration reads what
 the last one built.

 The mark sits ABOVE the keyboard poll rather than below it, so the
 bracket covers the whole iteration and not just the render. GopUsbKbd
 argues its idle path allocates nothing, and reading it that is true
 today; but on a board with a USB keyboard the pump runs a three-phase
 state machine on every frame, and the cost of this loop should not
 depend on a claim about another chapter that nothing re-checks. Only
 the scancode has to cross the restore, and an Integer is not heap.
 What the pump writes survives it either way: the ring, the report and
 the phase cell are fields built before the loop, and the mailbox at
 gk-cell is a fixed address.

  gsc-loop : R3dTriState, Scene3DState, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, Integer, UsbKbd -> [Device.Port] Integer
  gsc-loop (tgt) (scene) (sky) (base) (stride) (font) (ch) (kbd) = act
    let mark = __heap-save
    in act
      sc <- kbd-take kbd
      let ticks = peek-qword gsc-tick-cell 0
      in let angle = int-mod (ticks * 40) gsc-turn
      in let f = gsc-frame tgt scene angle sky base stride font ch
      in let dropped = __heap-restore mark
      in if sc == 1 then 0
      else gsc-loop tgt scene sky base stride font ch kbd
    end
  end

 The cursor is hidden for the whole run rather than saved under each
 frame: the view repaints continuously, so a save-under patch would be
 stale the instant it was taken and the first restore would stamp a
 ghost onto the scene.

  gsc-run : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, UsbKbd, Integer -> [Device.Port] Integer
  gsc-run (base) (w) (h) (stride) (font) (kbd) (cs) = act
    hd <- cursor-hide cs base stride
    let s = ui-scale w
    in let sky = rgb-to-packed (rgb 20 20 30)
    in let top = gsc-topbar-h * s
    in let cw = w - gsc-sidebar-w
    in let ch = h - gsc-taskbar-h * s - top
    in let tgt = r3d-target-new cw ch sky
    in let scene = gsc-scene 0
    in gsc-loop tgt scene sky (base + top * stride * 4) stride font ch kbd
  end

